00:40:17.000 --> 00:41:28.000
The gallows were the wooden scaffolding or frame used for execution by hanging. These gallows were erected in a purpose-built 30 square foot enclosure on the eastern side of the jail yard. The hanging was closed to the general public. Only ticket holders were granted access to view it. On January 14, 1881, 26-year-old Edward Reinhardt faced his final day. The day became a source of huge celebration across Staten Island, taverns all over celebrated from the early hours. People fought for tickets to see the hanging, but Mary Ann's family made no effort to attend. The night before, Reinhardt had stayed up until 5am, conversing with prison guards and reporters. He seemed cheery and joked with them, as if the day was not to be his last. Rain fell hard that morning, deterring many of the maddening crowd from attending. As time drew nearer, Reinhardt, although born Catholic, refused to visit from a Catholic priest, instead accepting a Baptist clergyman to remain with him until his moment of death.

